WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.870
I am still in the Arctic, so today’s guest
video comes from Collin Rodefer

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.700
over at the Collin Sphere Travel Vlog, who’s
reporting from China.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.950
Nice to meet you, my name is Collin and here
we are in beautiful Shanghai, China.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.650
Home to tens of millions of people,

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.440
Shanghai is one of the most populated cities
in the world.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.869
You might know some attractions that Shanghai
has to offer,

00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:20.550
such as the Bund, Nanjing Road,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.609
and even the newly opened Disneyland.

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:24.269
But despite the enormous population,

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:28.060
you might be surprised to find out that here
lies, within this city,

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.029
fully built newly constructed towns that remain
virtually empty.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:33.750
Modern day ghost towns.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.650
Located at the end of subway line 9 lies Thames
Town,

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:40.390
an English style ghost town with street names
such as Prince and Harry Street.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.330
It’s what you might expect from a replica
town which even sports many statues

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.080
of well-known figures from English history
and culture.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.390
This town was originally built to service
a thriving community.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.120
And with shop signs that read “open”,

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.790
you have to take a closer look to realise
that the fresh fruits

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.280
that are on display in the windows are actually
fake

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.810
and that these shots are, in fact, abandoned.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.840
On the chance that you do encounter people,

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.369
it’s not hard to see that this town serves
more as a place of tourism,

00:01:05.369 --> 00:01:08.659
and even more so, a backdrop for wedding photography.

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:12.250
Completed in 2006, Thames Town was one of
nine Western-style towns

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.590
to be built on the outskirts of Shanghai.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.889
In order to alleviate pressure and congestion
within the city centre.

00:01:17.889 --> 00:01:20.011
It was reported that it cost ¥5 billion to
complete,

00:01:20.011 --> 00:01:22.490
and can house up to 10,000 people.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.939
Its sole purpose was to house students and
staff at nine universities

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:28.479
in nearby Songjiang University City.

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:31.530
As you can tell, ten years later,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.650
it just hasn’t met that purpose.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.539
After walking the perimeter of the town,

00:01:35.539 --> 00:01:38.130
which only occupies one square kilometre of
space,

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.359
and seeing nothing but empty residential after
residential,

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:44.179
I decided to test my luck and head into the
city centre

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:47.170
to see what kind of lively events Thames Town
has to offer

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.520
on a summer’s weekend night.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:50.640
Nothing.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.530
So why did this happen?

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.530
Why did this fail?

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:54.530
Well, once the town was completed,

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.700
the majority of properties were bought up
by wealthy investors and individuals

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.100
looking to make businesses and this place
their second home.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.840
As naturally, as demand rises, prices rise,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.849
and in this case prices skyrocketed,

00:02:05.849 --> 00:02:08.850
making it unattainable for universities to
provide accommodation

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.290
for their students and staff.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.250
So with the majority of properties purchased,

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.610
you end up with a lot of empty shops, and
you have all the businesses

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.890
trying to provide services for around 1,000
people

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.670
when the population should really be around
ten times that.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:21.970
When you think of a ghost town,

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:25.030
you may think of the classic Western American
towns we all see in movies

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.470
with the ever-so-classic tumbleweeds being
blown into the distance.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.340
The difference between a town like that and
this,

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.230
is that those towns one point developed communities

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.280
and now we just have the remains of the buildings

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.950
– whereas here everything is new and clean,
and is just...

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.360
existing.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.560
Walking to this town, you definitely get an
eerie feeling here,

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.450
and it kind of almost feels like the Truman
Show,

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.650
as in you know there’s people living here,

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.000
however something about it just feels fake.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.370
The best way to describe it, I would say,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.250
is it kind of feels like a Hollywood set.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.990
But behind closed doors it doesn’t feel
so Hollywood,

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.110
and you get a totally different feeling.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:04.570
Searching around, I was able to get into a
small coffee shop called Andy Coffee,

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.160
and with the counters turned over and the
chairs thrown around,

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:10.300
you get the feeling that previous owners just
up-and-left the place.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.530
Like, they had to be suddenly evacuated.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:16.340
In that moment, I was rather paranoid about
being in there, but in hindsight,

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.840
I really should have just sat down and brewed
myself a coffee.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.480
The thought that I keep trying to shake is
that

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.820
this is an actual community where people actually
live their lives,

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.660
rather than just being a tourist attraction.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:32.290
And there’s reasons why there have been
many comparisons to the EPCOT Center,

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:36.480
and I have to keep reminding myself that I
can stay here as long as I want.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.120
There is no closing time.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.140
It’s… it’s quite interesting to say
the least, and would I live here?

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:43.140
Definitely.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.150
Definitely, if I could afford it.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.010
If you were to ask me how English this is
town actually feel,

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.580
I would say that other than the Chinese characters
around every corner,

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.760
and other tell-tale signs such as bamboo scaffolding,

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:57.310
you would have a hard time differentiating
between Thames Town and an actual English

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:58.310
town.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:03:59.770
Of course you can nitpick the small things,

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.760
like windows being larger in comparison,

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.420
but this is just a preference of Chinese culture.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.990
The middle ground lies somewhere in both the
English and Chinese undying love for tea.

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:12.560
Now whether this town is deemed successful
or not, that’s not my call.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.130
And although the town did not meet its initial
purpose,

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.670
the fact that the majority of the properties
were purchased,

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.650
coupled with the fact that China is now building
another one of these towns in Beijing,

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:25.840
tells me that they see something worthwhile
in building these little western towns.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:32.280
Once again, my name is Collin, and thank you
for experiencing this amazing place with me.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.280
Thank you, Collin!

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.280
Go subscribe to his channel now,

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:35.560
links are in the description or on screen.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.190
He’s got loads of great videos like that.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:40.800
And what I love about that is finding out
that the replica version of London

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.380
has exactly the same property bubble problem
as the real version of London.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:47.650
Next time, the final guest video:

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:52.370
and it’s about something that you’ve probably
seen and probably didn’t want to.

